---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const dataInventory = [
  { data: "Page HTML", where: "Browser memory", leaves: "Never", retention: "Discarded after parse" },
  { data: "Extracted reviews", where: "IndexedDB cache", leaves: "Never", retention: "7 days" },
  { data: "Review text", where: "Browser memory", leaves: "Never", retention: "Not stored at all" },
  { data: "Product identifier", where: "IndexedDB, hashed", leaves: "Never", retention: "With history entry" },
  { data: "Report and feature vector", where: "IndexedDB history", leaves: "Never", retention: "Until deleted, 500 cap" },
  { data: "Preferences", where: "chrome.storage.sync", leaves: "Only via the browser's own sync", retention: "Until removed" },
  { data: "Hashed reviewer prefixes", where: "Request body, opt in", leaves: "Yes, 4 hex characters, padded", retention: "Not logged" },
  { data: "Graph edges", where: "Request body, opt in", leaves: "Yes, hashed and delayed", retention: "Aggregated" },
  { data: "Crash reports", where: "Nowhere by default", leaves: "Only if enabled, opt in", retention: "30 days" },
];
---

<Layout title="Privacy" description="What leaves your browser when Verdict runs a check.">
  <h1>Privacy</h1>

  <h2>What leaves your browser</h2>
  <p>On a normal check: nothing.</p>
  <p>
    Verdict reads the page you already opened, analyses it on your machine using a model
    bundled with the extension, and stores the result locally. No request is made to us. This
    is verified by a test that fails our build if the extension makes any network request on
    the default path.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What we never receive, under any setting:</strong> the page you are on, the product
    you looked at, the review text, your identity, or anything that could be linked back to
    you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The one optional exception.</strong> If you turn on community checks, Verdict can
    ask whether any reviewer accounts on a page belong to networks flagged across many
    products. It asks using the first four characters of a hashed account identifier, mixed
    with random decoys, so we receive a set of buckets each containing thousands of accounts
    and cannot tell which ones you asked about, what the product was, or who you are. Requests
    carry no cookie and no identifier, and our servers do not log IP addresses. The deployment
    configuration proving that is in the public repository.
  </p>
  <p>This setting is off until you turn it on.</p>
  <p>
    Timing correlation is still theoretically possible for an adversary who controls both the
    network and the storefront. Verdict mitigates it with random request delay and decoy
    padding, and does not claim to eliminate it.
  </p>

  <h2>What data exists, and for how long</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="register">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Data</th>
          <th>Where it lives</th>
          <th>Leaves the device</th>
          <th>Retention</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {dataInventory.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.data}</td>
            <td>{row.where}</td>
            <td>{row.leaves}</td>
            <td>{row.retention}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What we will not build, ever</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No account system</li>
    <li>No advertising, and no affiliate links inserted into pages you are browsing</li>
    <li>No selling, sharing, or licensing of any behavioural data</li>
    <li>No telemetry that records which products or pages you visit</li>
    <li>No server side page fetching on your behalf</li>
    <li>
      No change to any of the above without a version bump, a changelog entry, and a notice
      inside the extension before it takes effect
    </li>
  </ul>
</Layout>

<style>
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
